  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "If $z=-8+2\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{\u2015}{z}.$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-8+2\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=-8,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{\u2015}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(-8) \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-8+2\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{\u2015}{z}=-8-2\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{\u2015}{z} & =(-8+2\\text{i})+(-8-2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(-8+(-8))+\\text{i}(2-2) \\\\  & =-16+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
